This is a change to the amdgpu driver to add some Barco adapter support.

You need to build separate kernel packages, per this URL:

https://canonical-kteam-docs.readthedocs-hosted.com/en/public/how-to/build-kernel.html

and use them to upgrade the CODE_DARK image we build.

This cannot be easily automated.

The amdgpu.c driver file gets the patch applied from the tree.
the build binary step.

Follow the instructions from the web page for getting all of the pre-requisite
packages installed (Installed required packages), but do not build the source
for the current running version.

The best way I've found to build this since the instructions are less than
clear and fundamentally incorrect in some ways, is to clone the current
leading edge of the noble (24.04) kernel development and build from that.
This will get you a definite better/newer version to boot and get you out
of trying to install the same revision on a single kernel revisions system
which is definitely not recommended (the instructions for adding a version
to the kernel build for the kernel source for the existing kernel version
do not, in fact, work at this time).

Follow these steps in this kernel subdirectory:

  git clone git://git.launchpad.net/~ubuntu-kernel/ubuntu/+source/linux/+git/noble
  cd noble
  git tag -l | grep Ubuntu | \
   grep <the end of the output of 'uname -r', i.e. trimming the '-' on forward>

This should get you a list of versions of which the current version you are running
under is closely related to. Pick one that is, based upon the number *after* the -
as the one you'll settle on.

Example- say you are running under Ubuntu 24.04.3. You are likely to have something like
6.14.0-27-generic as the version:

 orthanc@orthanc: $ uname -r
 6.14.0-27-generic
 orthanc@orthanc: $

So, search for similar branches with on the build machine in the checked out noble branch:

  mjacob@orthanc > git tag -l |grep Ubuntu |grep 6.14.0
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-15.15_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-22.22_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-23.23_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-24.24_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-24.24_24.04.2
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-24.24_24.04.3
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-25.25_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-27.27_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-28.28_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-29.29_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-32.32_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-33.33_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-34.34_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-35.35_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-36.36_24.04.1
  Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-37.37_24.04.1

The latest tag is therefore Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-37.37_24.04.1, 10 tags higher
than what a non-networked install of Ubuntu 24.04.03 has.

So, anything after 27 is good, but doing something like (which is what
my most recent build cache has) of:

  git checkout -b code_dark Ubuntu-hwe-6.14-6.14.0-32.32_24.04.1

works fine also.

After the checkout, update the amdgpu driver with our patch:

  patch -p1 < ../amdgpu_drv.c.diff
  git commit -am'add support for additional Barco adapters'

(obviously look for failure to patch correctly!).

Edit the changelog under debian.<version>, e.g. debian.hwe-6.14/changelog
so as to make a distinct ABI to make this different from the standard
release. Here is an example diff:

index 1ff980a1987c..a964fd32bbac 100644
--- a/debian.hwe-6.14/changelog
+++ b/debian.hwe-6.14/changelog
@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@
-linux-hwe-6.14 (6.14.0-32.32~24.04.1) noble; urgency=medium
+linux-hwe-6.14 (6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1) noble; urgency=medium

* noble/linux-hwe-6.14: 6.14.0-32.32~24.04.1 -proposed tracker (LP: #2120124)

Now you can just build more or less as documented, but here's
a cheat sheet:

  chmod a+x debian/rules
  chmod a+x debian/scripts/*
  chmod a+x debian/scripts/misc/*
  fakeroot debian/rules clean
  fakeroot debian/rules binary

Go out for lunch and to the gym- even on a fast system with an
SSD, it'll take a few hours.

When it completes, the directory above will have all of the requisite
packages. In the example case:

  mjacob@orthanc > ls -1 ../*.deb
  ../linux-buildinfo-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-cloud-tools-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-headers-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-hwe-6.14-cloud-tools-6.14.0-1030_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-hwe-6.14-headers-6.14.0-1030_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_all.deb
  ../linux-hwe-6.14-tools-6.14.0-1030_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-image-unsigned-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-lib-rust-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-extra-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-ipu6-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-ipu7-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-iwlwifi-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-usbio-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-modules-vision-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  ../linux-tools-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb

You only really need 3:

  linux-image-unsigned-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  linux-modules-extra-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb
  linux-modules-6.14.0-1030-generic_6.14.0-1030.32~24.04.1_amd64.deb

Put this into a tarball and when you are creating a gold master, you'll do
the following:

Clean /var/tmp:
  sudo rm -f /var/tmp/linux*.deb

Extra from your tarball those packages to /var/tmp and do:

  sudo apt install /var/tmp/linux*.deb

This should install and the next boot should give you a kernel with extra
Barco support which is the only reason this is being done at all.
